{
  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2023,
  "narrative": "The 2023 Texas Rangers arrived with championship ambitions and wasted no time proving they meant business. A blazing April run — capped by back-to-back heroics from Adolis García and Jonah Heim on April 11 — set the tone early, as Texas raced to a 16-11 mark to close the month. The offense was explosive, the rotation capable, and the belief in the clubhouse was palpable from the first warm days of spring.\n\nMay was the Rangers' best month, an 18-9 demolition of the league that announced Texas as a legitimate contender. Ezequiel Duran delivered clutch power, and the lineup's depth showed up night after night. June and July were bumpier — the bullpen, particularly Will Smith and Aroldis Chapman, proved to be a recurring headache — but the team's 14-13 June and 11-13 July were survivable. This was a squad too talented to spiral, even when the relief corps was lighting fires in the late innings.\n\nAugust brought the Rangers back to business at 15-12, fueled by Nathaniel Lowe's clutch doubles and a lineup that kept finding ways to win despite painful bullpen blowups. A September push at 15-13 wasn't sparkling, but it was enough. Texas had built a championship-caliber roster, and 90 wins and a playoff berth would be their launching pad for something far greater.\n\nOctober is where the 2023 Rangers wrote their legend. Surviving the Division Series, they navigated a tense ALCS against the Astros — weathering José Altuve home runs and José Leclerc hiccups — before Adolis García's three-run shot and Jonah Heim's clutch power pushed them through. In the World Series, Corey Seager's towering home run in Game 1 set the tone, and García — the heart and soul of this team all year — drove in the dagger blow. The franchise claimed its first World Series title, a coronation for a team that had been relentless, resilient, and ruthless when it mattered most.",
